Lossless, forever readable
description: representation and comments survive round-trips; fallbacks never drop data
- >
  What you wrote is what you keep. Comments survive; a scalar's spelling - quoted, hex, folded - is
  classified and preserved (the representation axis of the [concretes part](*::language:concretes)); a
  flow container re-emits as flow. Round-trip identity is the value *plus* its authored
  representation, normalized but never flattened.
- >
  Where a target form cannot hold a construct - flow refusing a bookmark, json5p refusing a tag -
  the serializer **falls back silently and losslessly** to a form that can: never a throw, never a
  drop. And a spelling, once valid, is read forever - the legacy `.yamlover` extension and the
  deprecated `~` back-edge sigil still parse, even though neither is ever written again. (The one
  deliberate pre-1.0 exception: the bracket operators `[]` and `[?]` were removed outright in
  favor of the `-` segment - they parse as errors that name the replacement.)
